1. `mkdir note`: make sure that `mkdir` will create only subdirectory 
    if a directory exist , if not then it throw error when parent directory
    doesn't exist in which which we wanted to create a sub directory. but to 
    bypass this we can use `-p` `mkdir -p new/first new/second` despite parent
    directory `new` doesn't exist this command will curate `new` first and then
    `first` and `second` as subdirectories in `new` using one command only.
    
    
2. `To get size of a file or folder in a human readable format through terminal`: 
    then use `ls -sh`.


3. `Creating Repository on github`: press on `+` from homepage of your github account 
    in which you wanted to create repo and then enter name , then switch on `readme.md` 
    and then click on `create repository` button. This will redirect to that repository
    newly created `test-git` .
    
    
4. `accessing a repo from github to local machine through git`: to access this through 
    git on our local machine go to main branch and then click on green `code` button 
    then click on `ssh` button and then copy url below `ssh` and then create a directory 
    in which you wanted to make a local copy of that desired github repository through 
    terminal and then open that directory in terminal and then type 
    `git clone git@github.com:Abhinav-Raj-Singh/git-test.git` `git` + `clone` + `url taken from ssh` 
    and then this will ask for password (if you set up password during creation of ssh key on your 
    machine) you set to access any directory on your local machine through your github account. 
    To in that currently pulled repository to our local machine and then run `git remote -v`
    and this will confirm by giving some ouput with your username.
    
    
5. `Basic git workflow`: we will see some basic and most used basic commands
	1. `git status`: once you did some editions like you created a file `hello_world.txt`
	under `git-test` and this `git status` will show this file in red colour under 
	`untracked files` section this means file is not staged
	2. `git add`: this will move your newly created file in the repo after cloning the 
	repo at local machine to `stage` section this section is like waiting room , means this 
	is pre step for committing. `git add hello_world.txt` and then `git status` then 
	`hello_world.txt` will show in green colour under `changes to be committed` section.
	3. `git commit -m "Add hello_world.txt` : by this command you have commited the file 
	and then `git status` nothing to commit working tree is clean you have successfully 
	commited. when you type `git status` you will get 
	`Your branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 1 commit.`  this means that you have newer
	snapshots on local repo as of remote repo.
	4. `git log`: typing this you will get all commits made on that repository.
	
	
6. `Modify a file or two`: open your `readme.md` file in vs code type `code .` and then 
    type something in `readme.md` and then save and then `ctrl + backtick` this will 
    open vs code terminal and then type `git status` and you will `changes not staged for commit` 
    and then `git add README.md` now `git status` we will see that file in green colour under
    `changes to be committed` and do changes to `hello_world.txt` and then save and then 
    `git add .` this will `stage` all the directories , subdirectories basically all the 
    content which is to be `staged` in our current working directory. and then 
    `git commit -m "Add hello_world.txt and README.md"` this will commit both the files. 
    and `q` to exist when stuck during checking `git log`.
    
    
7. `Push your work to github`: `git push` or `git push origin main` since we have only 
    main branch only then we can prefer only git push . and this will sync your remote 
    directory with latest work you did on that cloned repo in your local machine. and when 
    you do `git status` now 
    `Your branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.nothing to commit, working tree clean` 
    this means our repo is pushed back successfully.
    
    
8. `Avoid using github directly`: as a beginner direct don't interact with github interact 
    through local machine only because this could create trouble which would require advance 
    git knowledge to fix them.
    
    
9. `We are describing what new we did`: `git commit -m " hell_world.txt"` in this command 
    in double quotes we actually mention what we did with `hello_world.txt` file in this snapshot.
    
    
10. `program | action | destination`:This basic Git syntax is .


11. `Git best practices`: 
	1. `Atomic commits`: these are reffered to only one feature or task of your program so mark 
	`atomic commits` as `commit message` when you worked on something small because if something 
	get wrong that could be fixed and reverted easily.
	2. `changing the git commit message editor`: change you vs code default `commit` editor 
	because once you changed even if you missed `-m` flag that will be fine.and even once you 
	changed default editor in `vs code` then typing `git commit` will act as this will open 
	new window and then ask name for the commit and then press `commit` button there.
